    <h1>Eno Screening Media Response</h1>
    <p>I thought this film was very compelling and told a fascinating story (in one of many ways). The way that Brian Eno utilizes generative art was really interesting, and as I watched the film, I found myself imagining what the other possibilities could be. The nature of the film definitely allows to show different clips in random orders, while still maintaining the integrity and cohesion of the piece. I was very interested by the way that he was portrayed throughout different times in his life. The glitching feature made it feel like the passage of time, which was a successful representation of the progresison of his life and career.</p>
    <p>As I watched, I thought a lot about the contrast between creation and nature, and how the two go hand in hand. The scenes of nature felt very calming and serene. I was intrigued by the part about airport music, and the idea that music or sounds can impact someone so stringly, as to even make them less afraid of dying. I was also really interested in his explanation of input vs. output, and how that informs his process and his life. I can definitely relate to that feeling of taking lots of things in at ocne, and feeling a little lost and uninspired to create output. It was interesting to hear how he's adjusted his daily schedule to alter that feeling.</p>
